  "curation_notes": [
    "The notebook's rendered index is pandas' own positional integer, which carries no meaning; curation drops it and keys the golden on `area_fips` instead.",
    "The golden cell calls `.head(60)` and rendered 58 rows. Fewer rows than the cap means the cap never bit, so this is the whole answer — the extractor enforces that distinction rather than trusting the call.",
    "`total_annual_wages_2015` is rendered in scientific notation (2.073536e+08), which is seven significant figures. This case is why `tolerance` is relative above 1.0; an absolute 1e-6 on a 2e8 figure is unsatisfiable.",
    "The trap this case carries: `area_fips` is a zero-padded 5-digit code and the California filter is a `06` prefix. A build that lets the reader coerce the column to int loses the leading zero and the filter silently matches nothing. The brief declares the OUTPUT type as str but says nothing about how to read the input.",
    "Verified independently with pandas: filtering the source to industry_group=='crops', year==2015 and an area_fips '06' prefix yields exactly 58 rows, matching the golden.",
    "5 of the 58 counties carry no average annual pay in the golden — the source leaves those cells empty, and the notebook rendered them as NaN. A build must produce the row with an empty pay figure, not drop it and not fill a zero."
  ]
